1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,985
A new disk arrives, empty.

2
00:00:02,005 --> 00:00:06,924
Before you can store a single file on it, you have to divide it into partitions.

3
00:00:06,944 --> 00:00:10,257
Get this wrong, and you can erase a disk full of data.

4
00:00:10,727 --> 00:00:15,337
In this video: list, create, and delete partitions on GPT disks.

5
00:00:19,857 --> 00:00:22,440
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:22,910 --> 00:00:26,538
List, create, and delete partitions on GPT disks.

7
00:00:27,008 --> 00:00:31,774
We'll list the disks, put a GPT label on an empty disk, create two partitions,

8
00:00:31,794 --> 00:00:33,430
check them, and delete one.

9
00:00:42,950 --> 00:00:47,510
A disk is a long row of space. A partition is a named slice of it.

10
00:00:47,530 --> 00:00:50,563
A partition table, at the start of the disk,

11
00:00:50,583 --> 00:00:56,391
records where each slice begins and ends. GPT is the modern kind of partition table.

12
00:01:02,861 --> 00:01:05,303
Disks are named by the kernel.

13
00:01:05,323 --> 00:01:11,949
On this virtual machine, vda is the system disk, and vdb and vdc are empty disks.

14
00:01:11,969 --> 00:01:16,708
A partition adds a number: vdb1 is the first slice of vdb.

15
00:01:25,178 --> 00:01:27,667
The tool we use is parted. One warning:

16
00:01:27,687 --> 00:01:30,948
writing a new label erases the old partition table.

17
00:01:30,968 --> 00:01:33,329
Always check you have the right disk.

18
00:01:42,849 --> 00:01:48,973
First, list the block devices with lsblk. Each line is a disk or a partition.

19
00:01:48,993 --> 00:01:52,724
The disk vda has four partitions and is in use.

20
00:01:52,744 --> 00:01:57,272
vdb and vdc are five gigabyte disks with nothing on them.

21
00:01:57,292 --> 00:02:01,581
The tiny vdd is the setup disk of this lab; ignore it.

22
00:02:13,101 --> 00:02:18,163
Now parted, the disk, and the word print. It says: unrecognised disk label.

23
00:02:18,183 --> 00:02:21,550
The disk is blank, with no partition table at all.

24
00:02:33,070 --> 00:02:38,362
We create the label. parted -s runs silently, without asking questions.

25
00:02:38,382 --> 00:02:43,226
Then the disk, mklabel, and gpt. It prints nothing when it works.

26
00:02:51,696 --> 00:02:57,031
print again: Partition Table is gpt, and the list of partitions is still empty.

27
00:03:04,551 --> 00:03:08,219
Now a partition. mkpart, a name, a start, and an end.

28
00:03:08,239 --> 00:03:12,464
The first is called data1, from one mebibyte to one gibibyte.

29
00:03:12,484 --> 00:03:16,222
We start at one mebibyte, to leave room for the table.

30
00:03:24,692 --> 00:03:30,128
The second, data2, starts where the first ended, at one gibibyte, and ends at two.

31
00:03:30,148 --> 00:03:31,392
No gap, no overlap.

32
00:03:37,862 --> 00:03:41,831
print shows both partitions, with number, start, end, size and name.

33
00:03:49,351 --> 00:03:54,556
fdisk -l is another way to list partitions. Same two partitions, in sectors.

34
00:03:54,576 --> 00:03:59,438
The type says Linux filesystem, because we have not formatted them yet.

35
00:03:59,458 --> 00:04:01,363
That comes in a later video.

36
00:04:12,883 --> 00:04:16,319
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

37
00:04:17,839 --> 00:04:21,401
We reboot. Then lsblk shows the two partitions again:

38
00:04:21,421 --> 00:04:25,862
the partition table is written on the disk itself, so it survives.

39
00:04:35,382 --> 00:04:41,059
Now we delete partition two. parted, the disk, rm, and the partition number. Be careful:

40
00:04:41,079 --> 00:04:46,498
this removes the entry in the table, and the data in that slice becomes unreachable.

41
00:04:54,968 --> 00:04:58,724
print and lsblk confirm: only data1 is left.

42
00:05:10,244 --> 00:05:13,439
Your turn, on the disk vdc. Create a GPT label.

43
00:05:13,459 --> 00:05:18,158
Make a partition of five hundred mebibytes, and another one after it.

44
00:05:18,178 --> 00:05:21,168
Then delete the second one. Run the checker.

45
00:05:27,638 --> 00:05:29,389
Try before you open the solution.

46
00:05:34,909 --> 00:05:37,748
Next up: create and remove physical volumes.

47
00:05:38,218 --> 00:05:41,120
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
